  "result": "The O3 lesson is written, along with a short quiz and its key. I haven't opened the lesson in a browser or run the length check. I estimate roughly 3,700 words, inside the 2,700 to 6,750 range that fits 30 minutes.\n\n**Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`\n\nIt covers four things, each tied to the makerspace assistant:\n- **Context window:** what the model sees in one call, why you pay for it in tokens on every call, and why a bigger window isn't better.\n- **Between steps:** the context is rebuilt and resent each call, so a loop costs more than N times one call.\n- **Between sessions:** nothing survives unless the program saves a note and loads it back. It says what to save (short facts) and what not to (transcripts, calendar data a tool can give fresh).\n- **Retrieval:** chunking, keyword versus meaning search, and up-front versus on-demand retrieval. It also stresses checking the search first when an answer is wrong.\n\nIt includes three figures, a worked example and a sorting task, and a stretch question about halving the model bill. Each section has a practice check with instant feedback. The win is that you can sort the assistant's information into three places: always in context, saved notes, or retrieved on demand.\n\n**Quiz:** `quizzes/0002.json`, five items, ready when you are. Ask me for it and I'll grade it.\n\n**Source gap:** I only found secondary summaries of Anthropic's contextual retrieval numbers, so the lesson flags that and says to check the original. That gap is recorded in `RESOURCES.md`.\n\n**Misconception ids:** I assigned M7 and M8 for two new misconceptions, and recorded them in `NOTES.md`.\n\nQuizzes 0001 (s1 and s2) are still ungraded. Do you want me to grade them next?",
